KNOWNBUG
main.cpp
--cpp20
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ distinct addresses: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ e at offset 0: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.3\] line \d+ x at offset 1: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.4\] line \d+ size: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.5\] line \d+ WRONG \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^\*\* 1 of \d+ failed
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
--
The expected output is that of g++ 13 and clang++ 18.  CBMC places the
ordinary members first, at their sequential offsets, and the empty
[[no_unique_address]] members afterwards where no subobject of the same
type lies (place_empty_potentially_overlapping_members): x is at offset 0
and e at offset 1.  The program fails the second and third assertions.

Why FUTURE and not KNOWNBUG: the addresses are distinct, as
[intro.object]/10 requires, and the size is the same; which of the two
objects gets which offset is implementation-defined.  Only the Itanium
C++ ABI's choice is not matched.

What it would take: allocate in declaration order, moving an ordinary
member (and the members after it) when it conflicts with an empty
member allocated before it.  The ordinary members' offsets come from
add_padding, which knows nothing of the empty members, so this needs the
conflict-driven placement of ordinary members as well (Itanium 2.4 II.2:
"Place D at this offset unless doing so would result in two components
of the same type having the same offset").
